  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}{(9{x}^{2})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<x$\n\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThis given sequence is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r=9{x}^{2}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the sequence converges if \n\n$$\n0<9{x}^{2}\\le 1\\,\u27f9\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
